Well, the biggest realization you need is that when
you (s)earch, you aren't just searching the bit of
wall beside which your PC stands, you are searching
all eight grid cells adjacent to your PC, so in the
normal case, you're searching _three_ wall grid
cells at once. This in turn means that you can step
down the wall _three_ steps, do another search, and
you are seamlessly searching the wall. Using this
method, compared to searching every step or every
couple of steps will save you lots of turns
searching.

Another thing to realize is that sometimes the
problem isn't a door you haven't found in a wall,
but a continuation you haven't found in a corridor,
so you need to search the (whole) set of corridor
grid cells as well as the room cells, again taking
three steps between searching.

Also, once you've learned how the "random" room
layout usually ends up laying out the rooms in a
level, you can use that intuition to start your
searching on walls where the doors _probably_ are,
and only if that fails search where the door very
infrequently are. Similarly, listening for the sound
of money being counted will give you the clue that
there has to be room for a vault somewhere on the
level, and that is a four by four "vault plus
walls", plus sufficient solid rock beside it, a row
along each wall that could face into the rest of the
level (fewer if along an edge or in the corner of a
level); letting you guess directions in which
searching should be a low priority because they look
like a vault is over there.

You'll need to notice, under "places doors usually
aren't" above, that sometimes the corridor to a room
on the right can leave an existing room on the left
going left, and wrap around it to go right, but
that's the rare case.

Under "places doors certainly aren't" there's no use
searching a wall that is at some edge of the layer.

The strategy "pick-axe or dig-wand down, come back by
finding the up staircase" works as well in your
general case as it does for the Medusa level. Either
of those gadgets will also let you just plow through
the rock until you find a room or corridor, too. If
you have both, digging with a pick-axe (or broad
pick) is slower and expends more nutrition, favoring
using the wand of digging, but the wand of digging
can engrave a pretty durable Elbereth, favoring
pick-axe or broad pick use. NetHack is fun in large
part because of the challenge of managing resources,
this is one such point where that skill comes into
play.

Taking off all but one expendable piece of armor,
wielding an expendable weapon, dropping all your
scrolls and potions, picking up and reading one
unknown scroll at a time, if you have sufficient HP
to survive the possible results like a scroll of
fire (thus why you drop scrolls and potions before
reading each scroll) or a cursed scroll of
teleportation, might teleport you elsewhere on the
level to a part you haven't seen, or magic map the
level for you, and in the process perhaps enchant a
weapon or a bit of armor or destroy that bit of
expendable armor, thus identifying other useful or
dangerous scrolls where not much damage will result.

Lastly, _always_ remember that the down staircase
might be under something. I'm most usually fooled by
it being under a statue, but any object works to
hide a staircase. One way to minimize searching is
to be smart enough not to need to do it at all. You
need actually to do a ":", too, when looking under
objects; stepping onto an object that hides the
staircase is silent about the staircase until you
look down. Frankly, I think the DevTeam made NetHack
work that way out of sheer wickedness. One does have
to wonder why some pantheons of deities are
worshiped at all.

When you've spent a bunch of time searching,
remember that the RNG has used that same time to
build up a crowd of monsters somewhere out of sight
on the level, so advance with caution, probably
single stepping rather than using one of the long
move commands so that you can retreat one step to
wait at a corner to handle monsters one by one,
rather than stand facing an oncoming row of them
that are using missile attacks past each other to
get several attacks on you per turn.  It's hilarious
when they kill each other trying to attack you with
missiles or wands or spells, not nearly so funny
when you get hit by a crossbow bolt, an arrow,
several kinds of dagger, and a zap or two, in a
single turn. HP? What HP? DYWYPI?

